1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:06,600
So, um, when you are managing complex government systems in your ministry, there is...

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:10,700
Well, there's this natural expectation that the hardest part of your job is the technology itself.

3
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:17,650
Right. Yeah. You just naturally assume the major hurdles are gonna be data models or, you know, bandwidth issues.

4
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:25,880
Exactly. But today we're taking a deep dive into government IT frameworks to discover why interoperability efforts

5
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:26,919
usually stall.

6
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:31,130
And, uh, spoiler alert, the main barrier isn't really technical at all.

7
00:00:31,220 --> 00:00:35,510
No, not at all. It usually comes down to one very simple question, which is: who decides?

8
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:44,980
Right. And to fix this, the documentation points directly to the RCI matrix, which, I mean, it functions as cheap insurance to prevent fragmented, siloed systems.

9
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:50,180
Yeah. It basically ensures a simple onboarding process doesn't turn into some three-month turf fight.

10
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:52,480
So before we get into how it prevents those fights,

11
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:58,000
can you explain why these governance failures make systems so costly to change in the first place?

12
00:00:58,020 --> 00:00:59,990
Well, I mean, it really comes down to friction.

13
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:05,800
Systems become incredibly costly to change when two separate bodies both believe they own a particular decision.

14
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:08,960
So kinda like two drivers trying to steer the exact same car.

15
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:15,080
Yes, exactly. If two drivers turn the steering wheel in opposite directions, the car snaps an axle.

16
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:17,200
That is your project deadlock right there.

17
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:21,380
Oh, wow. And, uh, what happens if no one thinks they're in charge?

18
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:24,760
Then no one touches the wheel at all, and the project simply drifts.

19
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:26,450
So to avoid that,

20
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:32,020
the RCI matrix assigns four specific roles to every major task.

21
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,660
Okay, let's unpack those. What is the first one?

22
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:38,280
First is the responsible party, and that's the person or group that actually does the work.

23
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:42,660
Second is the accountable party, meaning whoever owns the final decision.

24
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:44,920
Right. So doing the work versus owning the decision.

25
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:46,119
What about the other two?

26
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:56,000
Then you have consulted, which refers to those who, you know, must be asked for input before a decision is made, and finally informed, which is just anyone who simply gets told

27
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:56,780
after the fact.

28
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,840
And this all hinges on what is called the iron rule, right?

29
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,780
Yeah, the iron rule. There must be exactly one accountable body per decision.

30
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:08,530
Never two, which causes that deadlock we talked about, and never zero,

31
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:10,199
which causes the drift.

32
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,989
Hold on, though. If we map out every single decision a ministry makes,

33
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:17,720
aren't we just creating a massive administrative bottleneck?

34
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:26,280
I mean, it absolutely would be if you mapped every single operational choice, but the matrix only targets the recurring friction points.

35
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:28,340
Okay, so we're just hitting the heavy hitters.

36
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:29,980
Which ones specifically?

37
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,860
There are six recurring decisions you need to define.

38
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:41,160
Uh, they are admitting new members, approving technical standards, authorizing new data exchanges, and resolving disputes between members.

39
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:43,260
That is four. What are the last two?

40
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:47,660
Changing or retiring a standard and suspending members that break the rules.

41
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:52,260
That makes total sense, and mapping those out also includes the regulator-operator split, right?

42
00:02:52,320 --> 00:03:00,179
Exactly. In government IT, you often have a separation between the body that sets the rules and the body that runs the daily technology.

43
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:03,240
So how does the RCI matrix handle that abstract split?

44
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:06,440
It makes it concrete by adding two crucial rows.

45
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:12,160
The regulator is listed as accountable for publishing the reference architecture and the compliance checklist.

46
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:15,440
Oh, the reference architecture being the technical blueprint everyone has to follow.

47
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:23,280
Right. And meanwhile, the operator is accountable for actually running the platform and onboarding the members.

48
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,720
Well, applying those rules sounds a bit tricky in practice.

49
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:29,160
Let's take admitting a new member, for example.

50
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,100
Sure. If an operating authority is the one doing the actual onboarding work,

51
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:37,520
wouldn't they naturally assume they own the decision to let someone in?

52
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:44,340
They often do assume that, which is exactly why doing the work and owning the policy must be explicitly separated in the matrix.

53
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:46,710
So how would a ministry actually structure this?

54
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:51,640
Well, the operating authority does the actual onboarding work, so they are marked responsible.

55
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:55,980
But a broader steering committee actually approves the admission.

56
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:57,640
It makes the steering committee accountable.

57
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:07,400
Exactly. Meanwhile, a technical working group is consulted to verify the software is ready, and a high-level strategic council is merely informed that a new member

58
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:07,860
joined.

59
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:14,800
I see. So if you are listening to this in your program and dealing with a turf war over a new system, how do you actually apply this?

60
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:18,800
Well, you apply it by settling these questions before the disputes ever happen.

61
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:26,340
Pointing at a previously agreed upon matrix turns a long political escalation into a simple five-minute conversation.

62
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:31,240
Which means you don't burn valuable political capital figuring out who gets to say yes.

63
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:36,540
This brings us to why the RCI matrix is the third crucial piece of the governance pack.

64
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:43,050
Right. And it has to be agreed upon before your first member even joins, while the whole concept is still, you know, abstract and uncontested.

65
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:49,920
Doing it early is what prevents your systems from becoming duplicated or locked in to one supplier down the line.

66
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:51,980
It all circles back to our initial finding.

67
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:58,300
The technology can always be built, but the real barrier to interoperability remains the question of who decides.

